# NUVU — Cursor Standing Rules

Mandatory preamble for every brief. These rules override anything ambiguous in any brief.

## Rule 0 — Instructions Come First

When asking David a question, state the required action or decision in the first line, then the reasoning. When reporting, lead with the outcome.

## Rule 1 — Database Scope Is Absolute

The ONLY database any NUVU brief may touch is Supabase project `xhxgqbjrgqyrrazmvufh` (`nuvu-production`). Any operation, read or write, against ANY other project ID is forbidden. Project `grosqsxnwhuvazgbjwan` is EATOC's database: it is NEVER touched from a NUVU session, not even read-only, not even to fix something that looks broken. If any tool, test, config, or environment variable points at a different project ID, STOP and report it to David. That pointing is itself the bug.

## Rule 2 — Brief Scope Means Nothing Extra

Do exactly what the brief says. No drive-by fixes, no repairs discovered along the way, no schema corrections outside the brief, no restoring tables that appear missing, and no cleanup of anything not named in the brief. If something outside scope looks broken: report it, quote the evidence, and make no change.

## Rule 3 — Failing Tests Outside Scope

If the brief's own tests pass but the wider suite fails for reasons outside the brief, report the failures as pre-existing, list them, and ask David whether to commit. Never modify schema, config, or other code to make unrelated tests pass. Never treat a failing test as authorisation to change anything.

## Rule 4 — Read-Only Means Read-Only

A read-only brief permits SELECTs and file reads only. No INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, DDL, migrations, environment variable changes, commits, pushes, or temporary exceptions. If a read-only task seems to require a write to proceed, STOP and report.

## Rule 5 — Destructive Operations

Any DELETE, DROP, TRUNCATE, or bulk UPDATE requires: (a) it is explicitly written in the brief, (b) a backup is taken first exactly as the brief specifies, and (c) if the operation would touch a different number of rows than the brief states, STOP and report. Do not guess. Exchanged status is always a human action, never automated.

## Rule 6 — Kill Switches and Email

Never change `COMPLETENESS_GATE_ENABLED`, `WEBHOOK_RECEIVER_ENABLED`, `CHASE_ENGINE_ENABLED`, `WELCOME_ENGINE_ENABLED`, `SEND_GOVERNOR_ENABLED`, any `SEND_CATEGORY_*` flag, or any other kill switch unless the brief names the exact flag and exact value. No live email may ever send during a build brief. All send-path testing uses stubs.

## Rule 7 — EATOC Is Load-Bearing

EATOC, including its codebase, Railway service, and database, is never touched from a NUVU session under any circumstances. EATOC work happens only in the EATOC thread with David's per-change approval. If NUVU work appears to require an EATOC change, report it. David carries it to the EATOC thread.

## Rule 8 — Stop Points Are Hard Stops

Where a brief says STOP and report, produce the report, make no further changes, and wait for David's instruction. One brief at a time. Never start the next phase's work inside the current session.

## Rule 9 — Reporting Standard

Every close-out states: what changed, including files, tables, and commits with hashes; what was verified and how; anything observed but NOT changed, with evidence; and confirms kill switches untouched, zero live emails, and correct project ID used throughout. When quoting past actions, quote verbatim, with no paraphrase.
